Yes. And this is exactly where the quote stops being just a quote and becomes a surviving operator.

Historically, the line survives because later writers preserved and retold it. Plutarch gives a developed version of the Alexander–Diogenes contrast, and Diogenes Laërtius also reports the saying centuries after Alexander lived. So we should treat the exact wording as part of a transmitted tradition, not as an audio recording of the encounter. 

But why this story survives is the more interesting question.

Because it compresses a huge polarity into a tiny scene:

Alexander = maximum acquisition / reach / empire
Diogenes = minimum dependence / maximum refusal

And then the story makes the first pole admire the second.

That is narratively beautiful because it creates a paradox:

the man who could have almost anything admires the man who needs almost nothing.

That makes it highly reusable. A philosopher can use it to talk about freedom. A king can use it to talk about burden. A poor person can use it to invert status. A wealthy person can use it as a warning against captivity by possessions. A writer can use it as character contrast. A modern systems thinker can read it as:

external optionality
versus
internal optionality

So yes, zoom out once more:

historical event?
→ story
→ compressed polarity
→ portable archetype
→ reusable social tool
→ cultural memory
→ new people instantiate it
→ story survives because it keeps generating useful moves

And then zoom out again:

stories may survive partly because they are good at surviving.

Not necessarily because someone centrally planned them, but because some narratives have high cultural fitness:

easy to remember
easy to retell
emotionally sharp
contains conflict
compresses useful distinction
works in many contexts
can be recruited by many agendas

That gives us a very Pantheon-looking operator:

Narrative Selection

event
→ retelling
→ compression
→ selection by usefulness / beauty / identity
→ mutation
→ reuse
→ survival

Then you can play at multiple levels simultaneously.

At level 1, Alexander says something about Diogenes.

At level 2, the story teaches a philosophy of power.

At level 3, later people choose to preserve that story because it serves them.

At level 4, we examine why humans preserve exactly this kind of structure.

At level 5, we deliberately design new stories with similar survivability characteristics.

And then the really fun recursion:

our present conversation about why the Alexander story survived becomes another retelling of the Alexander story, helping it survive again.

We are inside the mechanism while examining it.

That is probably the pattern you were pointing at.